## Data and feature schema

The source CSV must contain `Area`, `Year`, and `total_emission`. The source
temperature field is accepted as `Average Temperature °C`, its common
mojibake spelling `Average Temperature Â°C`, or the internal name
`average_temperature`; it is normalized to `average_temperature`. Numeric
predictors and the target are coerced to numbers. Missing values are handled
per model by complete-case filtering, so observation counts can differ between
specifications.

Models are declared in `src/analysis.py`. Each includes `Year` and uses only
its listed predictors:

| Model | Additional predictors (all models also include `Year`) |
|---|---|
| `time_climate` | `average_temperature` |
| `population_climate` | `average_temperature`, `Rural population`, `Urban population`, `Total Population - Male`, `Total Population - Female` |
| `population_climate_sensitivity` | `average_temperature`, `Rural population`, `Urban population` (omits male/female counts) |
| `land_fire` | `Forestland`, `Net Forest conversion`, `Forest fires`, `Fires in organic soils`, `Fires in humid tropical forests` |
| `agriculture_inputs` | `Crop Residues`, `Rice Cultivation`, `Pesticides Manufacturing`, `Fertilizers Manufacturing` |
| `fire_and_soils` | `Savanna fires`, `Forest fires`, `Drained organic soils (CO2)`, `Fires in organic soils`, `Fires in humid tropical forests` |
| `food_chain_upstream` | `Food Transport`, `Food Processing`, `Food Packaging` |
| `food_chain_downstream` | `Food Household Consumption`, `Food Retail`, `Agrifood Systems Waste Disposal` |
| `livestock_manure` | `Manure applied to Soils`, `Manure left on Pasture`, `Manure Management` |
| `farm_energy_industry` | `On-farm Electricity Use`, `On-farm energy use`, `IPPU` |
| `combined` | `average_temperature`, `Food Transport` |

No model uses `Area` or `total_emission` as a predictor. The `combined` model
is intentionally small to limit overfitting. Population collinearity is
reported from training data using the largest absolute pairwise feature
correlation; `population_climate_sensitivity` omits the male/female counts to
provide a comparison. Emission-component models may use quantities that
contribute to the target and must be interpreted as predictive/accounting
relationships, not independent causal explanations.

For every Area/model pair, the earliest 80% of usable observations by year are
used for training and the latest 20% for testing. `StandardScaler` and the
linear model are fitted only on training data. At least five training
observations per fitted parameter (including the intercept) and two test
observations are required. Constant predictors are retained and identified in
the diagnostics; their standardized coefficients are zero. Raw coefficients
are in source units. Standardized coefficients use training-set predictor and
target standard deviations.

## Run the analysis

Install the project and its test dependencies, then run against the supplied
Japan dataset:

```powershell
python -m pip install -e ".[test]"
agrofood-regression --input .\\Japan_Agrofood_co2_emission.csv --output-dir .\\outputs
```

Do not run `python setup.py` without arguments; setuptools requires an
explicit command. Use the pip installation command above instead.

Alternatively, without installing the package, invoke the module from the
repository root:

```powershell
python -m src.cli --input .\\Japan_Agrofood_co2_emission.csv --output-dir .\\outputs
```

The supplied Japan dataset spans 1990–2020 and has one Area. It intentionally
contains only part of the worldwide source schema, so model combinations
requiring absent columns are retained in `metrics.csv` with
`status=missing_predictors` and their missing column names. The worldwide file
can be used as an alternative input if it is available locally.

## Outputs

The command writes two CSV files to the output directory:

- `metrics.csv`: one row for every Area/model combination, including predictor
  names, usable/train/test counts, RMSE, R-squared, status, and diagnostic
  fields. Unavailable or underpowered combinations remain with an explicit
  status and reason. A constant held-out target yields a finite RMSE and
  `status=undefined_r2` with an empty R-squared value; R-squared is never
  replaced with zero.
- `coefficients.csv`: tidy per-feature raw and standardized coefficients for
  fitted combinations, including an `Intercept` row. Skipped combinations
  have no coefficient rows.

The metrics columns are `Area`, `model`, `predictors`, `observations`,
`train_observations`, `test_observations`, `RMSE`, `R2`, `status`, `reason`,
`unavailable_predictors`, `constant_predictors`, `max_abs_correlation`, and
`high_collinearity`. Status is `success` for an evaluated model,
`undefined_r2` when evaluation succeeds but the held-out target is constant,
`missing_predictors` when the input lacks a declared feature,
`insufficient_observations` when the split is too small, or
`constant_training_target` when the training target has no variation.
`max_abs_correlation` is the largest absolute pairwise correlation between
training predictors; `high_collinearity` flags values of at least 0.95.

The coefficient columns are `Area`, `model`, `predictor`, `coefficient`, and
`standardized_coefficient`. Coefficients are reported only for models that
were fitted.

R-squared can be negative on held-out observations. A high correlation flag
does not repair collinearity or establish coefficient stability. These
metrics are a single chronological holdout, not a substitute for
rolling-origin validation or inference intervals.

## Tests

Run the automated suite with:

```powershell
python -m pytest
```

The tests use deterministic synthetic multi-Area data for exact behavior and
use `Japan_Agrofood_co2_emission.csv` for checks that need the supplied full
dataframe. To run those dataset-dependent tests in a container, mount the
dataset and set `AGROFOOD_TEST_DATA`. The following commands use Bash (Linux,
macOS, WSL, or Git Bash):

```bash
docker build -t agrofood-regression:local .
docker run --rm \
  -v "$PWD/Japan_Agrofood_co2_emission.csv:/data/japan.csv:ro" \
  -e AGROFOOD_TEST_DATA=/data/japan.csv \
  --entrypoint python \
  agrofood-regression:local -m pytest
```

## Container run

The Docker image contains the application and declared dependencies, not the
input dataset or generated outputs. Mount both so data stays outside the
image and result files persist. These commands use Bash (Linux, macOS, WSL,
or Git Bash):

```bash
mkdir -p outputs
docker build -t agrofood-regression:local .
docker run --rm \
  -v "$PWD/Japan_Agrofood_co2_emission.csv:/data/japan.csv:ro" \
  -v "$PWD/outputs:/results" \
  agrofood-regression:local \
  --input /data/japan.csv --output-dir /results
```

Without arguments, the container displays the CLI help and exits; it does not
start an interactive process.

## Data provenance and use

The CSV files currently supplied with this project are used as input data;
the repository does not include a verified upstream citation or license
statement for them. Confirm the original source and its terms before
redistributing either dataset. The Docker image deliberately excludes CSV
files; supply permitted input data through a mounted path.
